      teach: [
        'Five kinds of systems work together on one order. The ERP knows what the customer wants and when. The MES spreads the work over lines and shifts. SCADA monitors the installation. The PLC runs the machine. And the HMI (human-machine interface), the touch screen on the machine, is where the operator sees and operates everything.\n\nThe PLC and SCADA were explained in the previous topic; here the point is what they pass to each other.',
        'It starts with a production order in the ERP: make 2,000 caps of type A, ready on Thursday. Two lists belong to that order.\n\nThe bill of materials (BOM) says which materials go into one product and how much: 25 grams of granulate per cap. The routing says which operations, in which order, on which work center and how long: injection moulding on machine 4, 12 seconds per cycle.',
        'Bill of materials and routing are master data: fixed information that comes back in many orders, such as part numbers, bills of materials and standard times. It is usually managed in the ERP and sent as a copy to the MES.\n\nIf the master data is wrong, everything after it is wrong. If it says 10 seconds cycle time while the machine manages 12, every shift looks too slow.',
        'Who keeps what differs per company, but usually like this: the ERP has the order, the bill of materials and the stock. The MES has the actual start and stop times, scrap with reasons, and which material batch went into which product. The PLC has the current settings and the counter. SCADA and the historian keep the measurements over time.',
        'What you hear in practice: “that’s in SAP” and “that’s in the machine” about the same number. Both can be true.\n\nTypical mistake: master data is copied once when the MES goes in and never maintained afterwards. Sharp question: “Who changes a cycle time when the machine gets faster, and in which system?”',
      ],
