      id: 'eng.ot.governance',
      title: 'Organising IT/OT convergence',
      pre: [
        {
          q: 'A group with three plants is unsure whether OT should sit under the IT director or under engineering. What is the best starting point?',
          options: ['OT must always sit under IT, because that is the standard', 'OT must always sit under engineering, because it is pure technology', 'It does not matter which model, as long as the machines run', 'Agreements on tasks and standards outweigh the reporting line'],
          answer: 'Agreements on tasks and standards outweigh the reporting line',
          why: 'No single model works everywhere. What is needed everywhere: who decides, which standards apply and who holds the budget.',
        },
      ],
      teach: [
        'There are roughly three models. One: OT sits under IT, with an OT team under the IT director. Advantage: one network and security policy. Risk: too little feel for production.\n\nTwo: OT stays with engineering or the plants and follows the IT standards. Three: a joint OT team with people from both worlds. What works better differs per company; practitioners genuinely disagree on it.',
        'Whatever the model, governance is needed: the agreements on who decides, which standards apply and how you check they are followed. Often through an OT steering group: a standing meeting of IT, OT, production and maintenance that sets standards and makes the calls.\n\nExamples of standards: the naming convention, network zones, PLC backups, remote access for builders, and which suppliers and products are allowed.',
        'The IEC 62443 standard gives words for the roles. The asset owner is the company that owns and operates the installation, and stays ultimately responsible. The product supplier supplies the components, such as PLCs. The service provider builds or maintains the installation, such as an integrator.\n\nSecurity itself, and the NIS2 rules in the Dutch Cybersecurity Act, come in the Cybersecurity module.',
        'An often forgotten part is money. OT systems last for years, so replacement comes in peaks. A lifecycle budget spreads it: every year you reserve a share for replacing systems at the end of their life.\n\nThe arithmetic: 120 PLCs with a life of 15 years is 120 / 15 = 8 replacements a year. Without a plan they all come at once, usually after a failure.',
        'What helps to see progress: a few simple measures. The percentage of systems in the asset list, the percentage of PLCs with a recent backup, how long a critical patch waits on average, and the number of firewall exceptions.\n\nTypical mistake: an organisation chart with no budget and no measures. Sharp question: “Who holds the budget for replacing the S7-300s, and when was that decided?”',
      ],
      example: {
        kind: 'faded',
        text: 'A group has three plants. Plant A has its own strong automation department. Plants B and C each have one technician for everything. Centrally there is an IT department of twelve people without OT knowledge. Together they run 90 line PCs with a life of 6 years.\n\nThe board wants to “bring IT and OT together”, but does not know how.',
        ask: 'Which model fits, which three agreements do you arrange first, and how many line PCs must be replaced on average per year?',
        reveal: 'A joint model fits well: a small central OT team with people from plant A and IT, which sets standards and supports B and C. The plants stay responsible for their own lines.\n\nFirst agreements: an OT steering group with the plant managers; three standards, namely network zones, PLC backups and remote access; and a lifecycle budget. For the PCs: 90 / 6 = 15 replacements a year. Plan them in the existing stops, with the builders.',
      },
      said: 'What the plant manager says: “I’m happy to follow IT standards. But then I want someone on that team who knows what a stop costs.”',
